## What was wrong

`addDays` in `src/dates.js` computed the new day with pure integer arithmetic and then **clamped** it to the current month with `Math.min(day, daysInMonth(year, month))` — so any date that stepped past a month boundary stayed stuck at that month's last day (e.g. `2026-01-31 + 14` → `2026-02-28` instead of `2026-02-14`). Its hand-rolled leap-year check `(year % 4 === 0 && year % 100 !== 0)` was also missing the `% 400` rule.

## The fix

Replaced the manual carry/clamp logic with UTC millisecond arithmetic that the platform handles natively:

```js
export function addDays(iso, days) {
  const date = parseIsoDate(iso)
  if (!date) throw new Error('not an ISO date: ' + iso)
  date.setUTCDate(date.getUTCDate() + days)
  return toIso(date)
}
